    <briefdescription>
<para>Idle-expiry cache for permission lookups, keyed by the generated permission query (which embeds the entity table and id). </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Each hit renews the entry&apos;s lifetime; an entry that is not used for the TTL is removed. The TTL comes from <ref refid="classConfigurator_1a32f2368e1e72e5bd29909e000c0c9e4c" kindref="member">Configurator::PermissionCacheConfig</ref> (PERMISSION_CACHE_TTL, default 300 seconds); 0 disables the cache.</para>
<para>Security trade-off: there is no invalidation on writes, so a revoked permission can keep being served while its entry stays in use. <ref refid="classBasicPermissionCache_1ad3230e761d0a35d3b772d299d8d72b09" kindref="member">invalidateAll()</ref> is available for callers that need an explicit flush. <verbatim>     All public operations are thread-safe via mutex_; generation_ is
</verbatim> atomic so readers can take a token without locking and have a racing <ref refid="classBasicPermissionCache_1abc720e1381076a0f4954ba9f6b39adfc" kindref="member">put()</ref> rejected. Think of it as a notepad: look up a question, and if the answer is still fresh use it; otherwise ask the database and jot the new answer down. <parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>ClockT</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para><ref refid="classBasicPermissionCache_1a3e99cd08033671e7ac34d935c0abc383" kindref="member">Clock</ref> used for idle expiry; defaults to std::chrono::steady_clock. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
